\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The inverse limit is a module of compatible cochains}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Fix #{N\ge 1} and let #{X_n} be the cycle, or the square torus,
of side #{2^n N}. Let #{s^*} be the dual of \ref{fdip-00OW} from
cochains on #{X_{n+1}} to cochains on #{X_n}. The inverse limit
is the #{k}-module of families #{(\alpha_n)} with
#{s^*\alpha_{n+1}=\alpha_n}. These are compatible cell
functions. The module is not identified with smooth forms. The
tower has no limit among finite calculi, and the tower is a
pro-object.}

\proof{
\p{Each #{s^*} is #{k}-linear. A family is compatible when it
lies in the kernel of the map
##{(\alpha_n)\mapsto(s^*\alpha_{n+1}-\alpha_n)_n}
between products of the cochain modules. That kernel is a
#{k}-module. Each value in a compatible family is the value of
a cell function at some finite stage, subject to the summation
rule on subdivided cells.}

\p{The vertex map #{x\mapsto 2x} includes the vertices of
#{X_n} into the vertices of #{X_{n+1}}. The direct limit of
these vertex sets is infinite. A finite calculus has a finite
vertex set, so no finite calculus is a limit of the tower. The
tower itself is the pro-object.}
}
